  "valuationInputs": [
    {
      "name": "Beta",
      "value": "0.98 (provisional)",
      "reason": "8 steps, 25 Nov 2026. (1) Business: a standalone primary dealer in Sri Lankan government securities (CAL Treasuries), funding a bond book with repos; profit comes from the spread and from trading gains and losses, so one sector: brokerage and investment banking. (2)–(3) A financial company, so no relevering: the sector's levered beta. Damodaran's emerging-markets file (betaEmerg) couldn't be opened from this run; his January 2026 global Brokerage & Investment Banking levered beta 0.98 is used, the same figure as for First Capital Holdings (CFVF.N0000, 21 Nov 2026). (4) Local 5-year betas: not looked up in this run; the share trades thinly (12.41% public holding, 86% held by the parent), so step 4 would set local betas aside anyway. (5) Higher of (3) and (4): 0.98. (6) Within 0.3–2. (7)–(8) Into scripts/reinvestment.py; shown as the Required return tile. Replaces the hand-set cost of equity (20% / 18.5% / 17%) of 13 Nov. Refresh in January 2027 with the emerging-markets figure.",
      "since": "2026-11-25"
    },
    {
      "name": "Cost of equity",
      "value": "Bear 19.8% / Base 18.3% / Bull 16.8%",
      "reason": "From scripts/reinvestment.py (25 Nov 2026): risk-free 8.24% (10-year bond 12.24% less 4.0% default spread) + 0.98 × (4.2% + 6.08% country premium) = 18.3%, ±1.5 points. Replaces the hand-set 20% / 18.5% / 17% of 13 Nov. No WACC: a dealer's repos and bank loans are its raw material, so it is valued on equity.",
      "since": "2026-11-25"
    },
    {
      "name": "Growth check",
      "value": "Assumed 5% / 6% / 7%; supported 0% on 2025/26 alone (payout 263%), 4.4% / 7.3% / 11.0% through the cycle (36.5% of profit kept over 2022/23–2025/26 × long-run ROE 12% / 20% / 30%). Bear 0.6 pts above; base and bull below.",
      "reason": "scripts/reinvestment.py on FY 2025/26 gives 0% because dividends of Rs 16.00 were 263% of EPS Rs 6.07. Over 2022/23–2025/26 equity rose from Rs 2.67bn to Rs 11.12bn with no new shares (stated capital Rs 723mn throughout, five-year summary p. 188) on profits of Rs 23.14bn (p. 187), so about 36.5% of profit was kept (calculated); × the long-run ROE gives the supported rate. The stated policy (CBSL Direction 01 of 2021, p. 182) caps dividends at 50% of the year's profit, which would mean keeping more, but the last two years paid 145% and 263% (p. 7), so the history is used.",
